1
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:08,410
Every financing request starts with
a business trying to move forward.

2
00:00:09,670 --> 00:00:12,750
I built the SQL engine behind
Merchant Credit Intelligence

3
00:00:12,790 --> 00:00:16,070
to explore what that business can support:

4
00:00:16,710 --> 00:00:20,970
short-term financing, repaid through
a fixed share of daily sales,

5
00:00:21,010 --> 00:00:22,950
using synthetic data.

6
00:00:24,150 --> 00:00:29,330
Power BI connects portfolio
patterns to individual decisions.

7
00:00:30,140 --> 00:00:32,350
Strategic Partner acquisition channel

8
00:00:32,390 --> 00:00:35,040
has eight funded accounts under stress,

9
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:37,920
and sixteen in Baseline.

10
00:00:39,150 --> 00:00:44,430
Both Power BI and Tableau present
the same SQL decision logic.

11
00:00:45,250 --> 00:00:48,170
Same applications side-by-side.

12
00:00:48,690 --> 00:00:52,130
Baseline versus Recession Energy stress.

13
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:55,340
I could have built this in Power BI too.

14
00:00:56,600 --> 00:01:00,840
I chose Tableau to demonstrate
my proficiency in both tools.

15
00:01:02,050 --> 00:01:06,950
Eleven Baseline approvals need
underwriting review under stress.

16
00:01:07,780 --> 00:01:10,460
Selecting the group
reveals the applications.

17
00:01:11,220 --> 00:01:16,200
Switching to selected-pairing view
shows its share of each industry.

18
00:01:16,690 --> 00:01:20,890
The tooltips keep the decision
pairing visible as we explore.

19
00:01:21,770 --> 00:01:24,430
Now, let's follow a different pairing.

20
00:01:25,150 --> 00:01:26,980
Here's merchant 98.

21
00:01:28,280 --> 00:01:31,580
$5,200 is authorized in Baseline.

22
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:33,840
$2,500 under stress.

23
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:37,340
Sales weaken and balances turn negative.

24
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:45,460
A smaller counteroffer keeps an option
open within the same 90-day term.

25
00:01:46,150 --> 00:01:48,820
Now let's follow the funded account.

26
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:55,960
The tooltip lets us inspect repayment
and remaining balances day by day.

27
00:01:56,470 --> 00:02:01,230
In Baseline, severe shortfall
leads to servicing review.

28
00:02:02,850 --> 00:02:07,670
In the stress simulation, the
smaller counteroffer reaches payoff.

29
00:02:08,050 --> 00:02:12,650
Two alternative simulations
followed all the way through.

30
00:02:13,750 --> 00:02:19,450
Merchant 258 needs specialist
counteroffer review in both scenarios.

31
00:02:20,090 --> 00:02:22,270
The system finds proposed terms,

32
00:02:22,570 --> 00:02:26,450
but finding an alternative doesn't
grant permission to fund it.

33
00:02:26,730 --> 00:02:29,370
That's why the account history is empty.

34
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:34,210
Both still need review,

35
00:02:34,610 --> 00:02:38,890
but the proposed amounts
are $10,600 apart.

36
00:02:39,650 --> 00:02:41,930
For a business considering its options,

37
00:02:42,310 --> 00:02:43,550
that difference matters.

38
00:02:44,430 --> 00:02:47,310
The narrative lets us compare the terms,

39
00:02:47,890 --> 00:02:52,930
liquidity, expected loss rate,
risk-adjusted contribution,

40
00:02:53,570 --> 00:02:55,350
and why review is still needed.

41
00:02:56,400 --> 00:02:59,580
Here we can follow repayment, monitoring,

42
00:02:59,880 --> 00:03:03,120
and payoff across scenarios and channels.

43
00:03:04,220 --> 00:03:09,320
In my first build, I questioned
monitoring signals that seemed too early.

44
00:03:10,240 --> 00:03:15,960
My human-in-the-loop analytical
leadership case study details that story.

45
00:03:18,250 --> 00:03:21,000
Three cases remain on review hold.

46
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:23,850
One is in active servicing.

47
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:29,750
Its payment history shows the return,
retry, and reconciliation.

48
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:31,550
Clear evidence.

49
00:03:31,900 --> 00:03:33,100
Thoughtful decisions.

50
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:36,350
I care about the merchant
behind the account

51
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:39,450
